1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,600
HOST: In 1550, the most powerful empire on earth did something no Christian empire had ever done.  Spain put its own conquests on trial. In Valladolid, a council of theologians and lawyers met to answer a single question: was the conquest of the Americas just, or was it a crime?

2
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:43,600
HOST: By the end of this lesson, you will be able to do three things. One: summarize Sepúlveda's argument, that some peoples are slaves by nature. Two: explain Las Casas's defense of Indigenous peoples, and his own tragic irony. Three: evaluate why the debate changed little on the ground, yet mattered for the history of rights.

3
00:00:43,600 --> 00:01:11,000
HOST: For the conquest: Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda. Scholar. Logician. He had never set foot in the Americas. Never seen a single person he was about to describe.
SEPULVEDA: Aristotle settled this two thousand years ago. Some peoples are slaves by nature: born to be ruled, unfit to govern themselves. Their idolatry, their human sacrifices: these offend nature itself. A war that ends such practices is not cruelty.

4
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:16,799
SEPULVEDA: It is mercy. We bring them the faith. We bring them law.

5
00:01:16,799 --> 00:01:28,599
HOST: Against him: Bartolomé de las Casas. A Dominican friar.  Fifty years in the Indies. When the council gave him the floor, he held it for five days.

6
00:01:28,599 --> 00:01:43,199
LASCASAS: My opponent brought Aristotle. I brought fifty years. He has read about the Indies. I lived there. They are not natural slaves. They are rational people, capable of the faith, capable of governing themselves.

7
00:01:43,199 --> 00:01:48,599
LASCASAS: What I witnessed was not native barbarism. It was Spanish cruelty.

8
00:01:48,599 --> 00:01:53,199
HOST: Day one. Day three. Day five.  He finally stopped.

9
00:01:53,199 --> 00:01:59,799
HOST: Now, you're the council. The logician with Aristotle, or the eyewitness with fifty years?

10
00:01:59,799 --> 00:02:07,199
HOST: The real judges never decided. No verdict. Both men claimed victory. And the conquest went on.

11
00:02:07,199 --> 00:02:37,000
HOST: So the debate changed nothing. And everything. Before Valladolid, no empire had formally asked whether winning a war could still make it wrong. Las Casas wrote the question down. The same man who once proposed African slavery to spare the Indians, then spent his life regretting it. The empire is gone. But the idea survived: conquest itself could be judged by universal moral law[/slow], the language of natural rights and human dignity.

12
00:02:37,000 --> 00:03:16,800
HOST: Here is the hardest part of his story. Years before Valladolid, Las Casas himself had proposed bringing enslaved Africans to the Indies, hoping to spare Indigenous workers.  He spent the rest of his life regretting it. In his own history of the Indies, he wrote that he regretted the advice bitterly, and blamed himself for not seeing what it would become.  The man who defended one people had helped condemn another. That is why his defense matters: it was not written by a saint. It was written by a man who knew exactly what guilt felt like.

13
00:03:16,800 --> 00:03:23,800
HOST: Every time we ask it about a war today, we're still sitting in that room.
